---
/* RIPOSTO — the lower town, and where the search stands.
 *
 * This is a page about work in progress, which the archive usually keeps on
 * the work list rather than on a town page. It is here because Riposto is the
 * only comune left that fits Giovanni Nicotra, and a reader who follows the
 * Nicotra line arrives at a question rather than an answer. Saying so is the
 * honest end of the thread.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import holdings from "../data/holdings.json";
import coverage from "../data/coverage.json";
import nic from "../data/nicotra.json";

const HERE = "Riposto";
const rows = holdings.rows.filter((r) => r.comune.startsWith(HERE));
const cov = coverage.rows.find((r) => r.comune === HERE) || {};
const vols = rows.reduce((n, r) => n + r.volumes, 0);
const bought = rows.reduce((n, r) => n + r.bought, 0);
const SER = nic.byComuneSeries["Riposto"] || {};
const BIRTHS = SER["Nati"] || [];
const MARRS = SER["Matrimoni"] || [];

const HOUSEHOLDS = nic.housesByComune["Riposto"] || [];
---
<Base title="Riposto" eyebrow="Catania, Sicily"
      dek="The lower town, separate from Giarre since 1821 — and the only comune left that fits the man this archive cannot find.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">21–22 September 2026 · where the search stands</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Why this town, and not another</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p><strong>Giovanni Nicotra</strong> is the last unfound person on the Sicilian spine. His own
      marriage certificate says he was <em>nato a</em> <a href={u("/giarre/")}>Giarre</a>;
      <strong>eleven Giarre birth years, read end to end in both the main run and the appendix, do not
      hold him</strong>. <a href={u("/mascali/")}>Mascali</a>, where he was living in 1872, does not
      hold him either — nine years read there.</p>
      <p>Riposto is the lower town, the port below Giarre, and <strong>it has registered separately
      since 1821</strong> — through the whole window in which he was born. A man of the lower town
      could say <em>Giarre</em> and be entered here. <strong>That is a reading from general history
      and not from any document</strong>, and it is written down as such.</p>
      <p>What makes it more than a guess is what the registers hold. <strong>Giarre's only Nicotra
      across eleven years is a <em>Dottor Don</em> with a <em>Donna</em> wife.</strong> Mascali's
      across nine is an Alfio. <strong>Riposto has three ordinary Nicotra households</strong> — the
      sort of family a bracciante belongs to.</p>
      <p>And in September 2026 the reading stopped being an inference. <strong>Riposto's marriage
      Tavola for 1844 records Nicotra Salvadore, <em>patria Giarre</em>, marrying a Riposto
      woman</strong> — and his two children then appear in Riposto's own birth Tavole, in 1845 and
      1847. A Nicotra who says <em>Giarre</em> and is registered here is no longer a theory about how
      people moved; it is a man with an act number.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The Nicotra of Riposto</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:12px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Father</th><th>Mother</th><th class="m">Seen</th><th>The children</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {HOUSEHOLDS.map((h) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{h.father}</strong></td><td>{h.mother}</td>
                <td class="m small">{h.seen}</td><td class="small">{h.note}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><strong>None of them is Rosario.</strong> Giovanni
    Nicotra's father is named in the 1872 act as <strong>Rosario</strong>, a bracciante of Mascali,
    with <strong>Venera Murabito</strong> — and no household of that name has been found in any of the
    three towns. Four Riposto years remain unread.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Every birth year read, and what it said</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.7rem 0 1.2rem">Riposto's <em>Tavola</em> is alphabetical and names
    both parents. Each N section was checked into the next letter before anything was written down.
    <strong>Eleven consecutive years, 1838 to 1848, are now read end to end.</strong></p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Year</th><th class="m">N entries</th><th>What is there</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {BIRTHS.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="m"><strong>{r.year}</strong></td>
              <td class="m small">{r.entries === "—" ? <em>unread</em> : r.entries}</td>
              <td class="small">{r.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><strong>1841 was the one unfinished negative, and it is
    finished now.</strong> The first volume's alphabet runs M into P with its last leaf torn and the
    right page lost — and Giarre had already taught this archive that an appendix can sit past the end
    of the alphabet. <strong>There is a second 1841 volume</strong> (<em>17517, suppl. 2</em>), and it
    carries its own untorn <em>Tavola</em>: M runs into O with <strong>no N heading at all</strong>.
    The O it holds is exactly what the torn leaf lost. Two volumes for one year is not two witnesses —
    a duplicate is a copy — but the copy reaches the part the original cannot show.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And then the marriages, which were the better instrument</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.7rem 0 1.2rem">Twelve years, 1835 to 1846. The marriage
    <em>Tavola</em> names both spouses, <strong>all four parents, and the comune each spouse came
    from</strong> — so a single row can carry a whole generation.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Year</th><th class="m">N entries</th><th>What is there</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {MARRS.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="m"><strong>{r.year}</strong></td>
              <td class="m small">{r.entries === "—" ? <em>unread</em> : r.entries}</td>
              <td class="small">{r.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <figure style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/riposto1844-matr39-nicotra-giarre.jpg")} alt="Riposto, Matrimoni 1844, atto 39 — Nicotra Salvadore, patria Giarre"
           style="width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid var(--rule)" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">Riposto, Stato civile della restaurazione, <strong>Matrimoni 1844</strong>, the annual <em>Tavola</em>, <strong>atto 39</strong> — Archivio di Stato di Catania (Antenati <em>an_ua73136</em>, image 96). Six columns: the spouses, <strong>the patria of each</strong>, all four parents, and the date. <strong>Nicotra Salvadore — Giarre — Nicotra Alfio / Pappalardo Rosa</strong>, marrying <strong>Barbagallo Maria — Riposto — Barbagallo Rosario / Campagna Anna</strong>, in July 1844. The day of the month is not legible in the index; the act itself would give it.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><strong>1844 is the one that pays.</strong> Atto 39 marries
    <strong>Nicotra Salvadore</strong> to Maria Barbagallo of Riposto — and the <em>patria</em> column
    gives his as <strong>GIARRE</strong>, with his parents named as <strong>Alfio Nicotra and Rosa
    Pappalardo</strong>. He is the father of the two Riposto children the birth sweep had already
    turned up, in 1845 and 1847. <strong>Here is a Nicotra born at Giarre, married at Riposto, and
    registering his children at Riposto</strong> — which is precisely the shape this archive had
    guessed at for Giovanni, and had no document for until now.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What Riposto holds</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.7rem 0 1.2rem">{vols} volumes, facet total {vols} — complete in one
    page load, comfortably under the ceiling. {bought} openable now.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Fondo</th><th>Record type</th><th class="m">Years</th><th class="m">Volumes</th><th class="m">Openable</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="small">{r.fondo}</td>
              <td><strong>{r.type}</strong></td>
              <td class="m small">{r.from ? `${r.from}–${r.to}` : "—"}</td>
              <td class="m">{r.volumes}</td>
              <td class="m small">{r.bought || "—"}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px">The restauration run stops at <strong>1860</strong> and the
    Italian one begins at <strong>1866</strong> — <strong>a five-year hole</strong> in the middle,
    1861 to 1865, which is the unification gap this archive has met at other comuni. It matters here
    only if the answer turns out to lie in it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What would end this</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.8rem 0 1.2rem">Twenty-three Riposto years are read and Rosario is in
    none of them. That is now a finding rather than a gap.</p>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <ul style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;line-height:1.75;color:var(--ink-2);padding-left:20px">
        <li><strong>The Giarre marriages.</strong> Salvadore Nicotra was <em>born</em> at Giarre and
        married at Riposto. If Rosario is his brother — and nothing yet says he is — then Rosario's own
        marriage to Venera Murabito is most likely to sit in <strong>Giarre's or Mascali's</strong>
        marriage Tavole, neither of which has been swept.</li>
        <li><strong>Salvadore Nicotra's own birth act at Giarre.</strong> He married in 1844, so he was
        born about 1815–1824 — <strong>before every Giarre year this archive has read</strong>. That
        act would name Alfio Nicotra's age and trade, and would say whether this is a bracciante
        family or the <em>Dottor Don</em> one.</li>
        <li><strong>The deaths.</strong> Rosario and Venera were alive in October 1872 and no death
        has been sought for either, in any of the four towns.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><a href={u("/nicotra/")}>The Nicotra line →</a> · <a href={u("/worklist/")}>The work list →</a> ·
      <a href={u("/giarre/")}>Giarre →</a> · <a href={u("/mascali/")}>Mascali →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
